    <section class="section">
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>This summary reports against the approved ATLAS Scope of Work for Parachute. There are two versions of that scope:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>v1 (2026-01-21)</strong> &mdash; the initial Q1 roadmap, <a href="/archive/roadmap-v1.html">archived at parachute.computer/archive/roadmap-v1.html</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>v2 (current Spring 2026)</strong> &mdash; the <a href="/roadmap/">current roadmap</a>, which evolved through biweekly reviews with my advisor across the semester.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Both versions are public and part of the project's record. This report indexes on <strong>v2</strong> as the live scope while noting where v1 set an earlier expectation that the project later revisited. Parachute has been open and local-first throughout; what changed mid-semester was the <em>shape</em> &mdash; from a three-surface agentic stack (Daily, Pro, Computer) to a central knowledge-graph primitive (Vault) accessible to any AI over MCP, with a voice-first capture client (Daily) as one way in.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Vision</h2>
        <blockquote class="sow-quote">Parachute is an open knowledge layer for humans and AI to think and remember together. A personal knowledge graph organized around notes, tags, and links &mdash; yours, on your machine.</blockquote>
        <p><strong>Parachute Vault</strong> is the core. It's a self-hosted knowledge graph exposed over MCP to any AI client and over a REST API to other applications. One command installs it.</p>
        <p><strong>Parachute Daily</strong> is a voice-first journaling interface on top of the Vault, for iOS, Android, and macOS. Local transcription, offline-capable, syncs to your vault through the API when connected. It's one interface among many that can exist over the same Vault &mdash; the user chooses how they want to put thoughts in and get them back out.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>The mid-semester shape shift</h2>
        <p>v1 proposed three surfaces:</p>
        <ol class="sow-ordered">
            <li><strong>Parachute Daily (hosted)</strong> &mdash; a voice journaling app with cloud backend.</li>
            <li><strong>Parachute Pro</strong> &mdash; the full experience app.</li>
            <li><strong>Parachute Computer</strong> &mdash; a heavyweight self-hostable agentic server (Chat + Vault + Daily + modules + trust-tier sandboxing).</li>
        </ol>
        <p>By late March it became clear this was more platform than primitive &mdash; it asked users to adopt a whole ecosystem rather than slot into the tools they already use. The rewrite that followed:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Reframed <strong>Parachute Vault</strong> as the focused standalone primitive: simple REST API, 9 MCP tools, one command to install. Any MCP-capable AI client &mdash; Claude Code, Claude Desktop, third-party tools &mdash; connects over MCP. Other applications (including Daily) connect over the REST API. No requirement to adopt a full Parachute agent stack.</li>
            <li>Reframed <strong>Parachute Daily</strong> as a voice-first interface on top of the Vault, rather than a standalone journaling product with its own backend.</li>
            <li>The "Parachute Computer" name now refers to the <strong>ecosystem umbrella</strong> (the GitHub organization and surrounding projects) rather than a single installable server.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The v1 MRR target &mdash; $1,000+ / 200&ndash;1000 paying users by 4/22 &mdash; is not met under this shift. The revenue path is now a tiered add-on model aligned with the Vault's OSS distribution, and first paying users are a post-launch deliverable rather than a semester metric.</p>
    </section>

