    <section class="section">
        <h2>Reflection</h2>
        <p>The load-bearing decision of the semester was reshaping the project in late March &mdash; away from a three-surface agentic stack and toward the Vault as a single, focused primitive that any AI can talk to. The real difference was <strong>what we were asking users to adopt</strong>: a whole new ecosystem, or a vault that slots in alongside the AI tools they already use. The second is easier to install, easier to explain, and more durable against the AI landscape continuing to change week to week. The cost: the MRR targets from the January plan are not met by the semester's end, because the new shape reaches users first through open-source adoption rather than hosted subscriptions.</p>
        <p>At presentation time:</p>
        <ol class="sow-ordered">
            <li><strong>Parachute Vault is real and live on npm.</strong> One command installs it. Five beta users are already running it. Open to any MCP-capable AI.</li>
            <li><strong>Parachute Daily</strong> &mdash; voice-first capture on top of the Vault &mdash; is ready on iOS, Android, and macOS and entering wider beta.</li>
            <li><strong>The pendant prototype is built</strong> &mdash; PCB and 3D-printed enclosure &mdash; with audio-to-vault integration as the active work through Creative Technologies class.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The project ships from the semester as a thing people can install and use, not a plan they can read.</p>
